title: Why a wiki
tags: Development
kind: rationale
summary: Why the documentation is one wiki of small notes, with its mechanical half generated from the code — and what it replaced.
audience: dev

Until October 2026 the documentation was a set of long specifications
(`docs/spec-*.org`, one per subject), 27 hand-written HTML pages inside the
`help` panel, and the help texts of `mf`. The three said the same things in
different words, drifted apart, and drifted from the code: checked against the code
subject by subject during the move, nearly every specification described something
that had since changed. The wiki replaced them ([[Writing documentation]] says how
it is written). Cross-repository sync was the last subject moved (October 2026):
checked against the code, its specification described more than was built and some
behaviour that was wrong, which was fixed before the notes were written ([[Sync]]).

!! One source, several readers

The wiki under `docs/wiki/` is the only source. Its rendering belongs to no panel:
it is installed under `~/.config/metafolder/docs/`, where the [[help panel]] reads it
and where any other reader — a web site, a command — could. The rendering is a
build product and is not committed, like the frontend's bundle;
`metafolder-sync-config` refuses to run without it, because applying the
configuration without it would empty the help.

The developer documentation is in the same wiki and ships with the rest: a note
says `audience: dev`, and the help leaves those out of a search unless asked. One
place to look, and no second tree to keep in step.

!! One note per question

A specification is read from the top; documentation is mostly looked up. So a note
answers one question, a topic is a short overview note listing its notes by kind
(how to, what is true, why), and the linear reading of a topic is assembled at
render time rather than kept as a file. The files are flat under `tiddlers/` — tags,
not folders, say what a note belongs to, so a note touching three topics has no
wrong place to live.

!! The mechanical half comes from the code

Lists written by hand — every command, every route, every reserved field — go stale
the day something is added. Six catalogs are generated from the code instead, and
committed, so a change of the command line shows in the diff of the documentation
and can be read without a build. Every catalog is enforced: an item of the code
with no note fails the check, which is what makes "every command is documented"
true rather than hoped. A note is still written by hand for each item (even a
trivial GUI command), because the generated part only knows what the code knows.

!! Close to plain text

The notes use a small subset of TiddlyWiki's wikitext, refused otherwise by the
check, and every list and procedure is expanded when rendering. So a `.tid` file
reads almost as text, the panel stays a small reader of HTML fragments that knows
nothing of TiddlyWiki, and `scripts/doc` reads the files directly — no TiddlyWiki
started, answers at once — for a person or an agent working from the command line.

!! Decided along the way

* No `mf doc` for now: the help panel and `scripts/doc` are the readers.
* A citation from the code is `doc "Title"`, anywhere under `crates/` or `scripts/`, comment or not — hence the check's name in `scripts/check.sh` is `wiki`, not `doc` (a `skip doc "install node"` line was itself a citation).
* Design decisions kept in an agent's memory files became `rationale` notes for developers, topic by topic; the memory keeps only environment traps and methods.
